 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/De6n8wQCiAR/",
 "title": "5-Step Persuasion Formula for a Great Sales Pitch",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "Mark Satterfield explains that a great sales pitch is not about finding the perfect words, but about putting the right ideas in the right order. He outlines a 5-step persuasion formula to help you convince prospects that the problem is worth solving before presenting your solution.",
 "key_points": [
  "First, identify the problem that is actually costing your prospect money, time, or peace of mind, rather than just the obvious problem.",
  "Second, make the consequences real by examining what happens if they do not solve the problem and determining the cost of doing nothing.",
  "Third, introduce a different way of thinking by giving prospects an insight that makes them reconsider how they have been approaching the problem.",
  "Fourth, provide proof through a client story, demonstration, or specific example that makes your solution believable.",
  "Fifth, offer a clear next step by telling prospects exactly what to do and why it makes sense to do it right now.",
  "Do not start selling your solution before you have convinced someone that the problem is worth solving, as that is where most pitches fail."
 ],
 "content": "Most people think a great sales pitch is about finding the perfect words. It's really not. It's about putting the right ideas in the right order and whether you're selling a $500 product or a $50,000 service, there's a persuasion formula you can use that's very effective. First, identify the problem, not the obvious problem, but the one that's actually costing your prospect money, time, or peace of mind. Second, make the consequences real. What happens if they don't solve it? What's the cost of doing nothing? Third, introduce a different way of thinking. Give them an insight that makes them reconsider how they've been approaching the problem. Fourth, provide proof, a client story, a demonstration, or a specific example that makes your solution believable and fifth, offer a clear next step. Tell them exactly what to do and why it makes sense to do it now. Now, here's the important part. Don't start selling your solution before you've convinced someone that the problem is worth solving. That's where most pitches actually fall apart. So, I developed a program that walks you through how to put together a persuasive pitch using the same principles behind successful direct response advertising and sales presentation. So, if you'd like to learn how to create a pitch that gets more people to say yes, go ahead and comment pitch and I'll send you the information. This is Mark Satterfield. Hope you found this helpful. Please like and subscribe if you haven't already and of course, I always appreciate you passing these videos along to others you know who are marketing their products or services to the affluent. I'll talk to you soon. Bye for now.",
